<h1>Orcraft Test Step</h1>
<p>A step is the smallest unit of work in Orcraft: one action, its inputs, its outputs. Steps exist for
  two reasons. They make a long case body readable by naming its parts, and they are the only component
  that can be dispatched to a remote endpoint - which is what turns a local test into a distributed
  one.</p>

<p>Like every component, a step is three classes:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

import os

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestStep,
	OrcraftTestStepConfig,
	OrcraftTestStepResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
	OrcraftResultField,
)


class MyStepConfig(OrcraftTestStepConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Step")
	output_folder = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="", label="Output Folder")
	run_id = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="", label="Run Id")


class MyStepResult(OrcraftTestStepResult):
	output_file = OrcraftResultField.Text(default="", label="Output File")


class MyStep(OrcraftTestStep):
	def __init__(self, config: MyStepConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or MyStepConfig())

	def _run_step(self) -> OrcraftTestStepResult:
		self.result = MyStepResult()

		output_file = os.path.join(self.config.output_folder, f"{self.config.run_id}.dat")
		self.result.output_file = output_file

		was_removed = os.path.exists(output_file)
		if was_removed:
			os.remove(output_file)

		self.checkpoint(
			True,
			f"Stale output removed: {output_file}" if was_removed else "No stale output to remove.",
			"",
		)

		return self.result') }}

<h2 id="run-step">The step body</h2>
<p><code>_run_step</code> is the only method a step must implement. It creates its result, does the work,
  and returns the result - and the returned object must be an <code>OrcraftTestStepResult</code>, which
  the framework checks rather than letting a mistyped return surface later as a missing attribute.</p>

<p>A step is not handed a case. That is the point: it receives everything through its config and reports
  everything through its result, with no access to the test around it. Keeping to that discipline is what
  lets the same step run locally during development and on a remote endpoint in a distributed run,
  unchanged.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Step config fields</h2>
<p>Beyond <code>name</code>, <code>description</code> and <code>log_orientation</code>, a step config
  inherits two fields of its own:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>target_endpoint</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>The endpoint the step is dispatched to. Set by the flow that runs it, not usually by hand.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>expect_endpoint_disconnect</code></td>
      <td><code>False</code></td>
      <td>Marks a step that is <em>supposed</em> to take its endpoint offline - a reboot, a service restart - so the disconnection is not reported as an error.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="granularity">How small is a step</h2>
<p>A useful step is one action that can succeed or fail on its own terms: delete a file, launch a process,
  read a register, transfer a build. If a step needs two checkpoints about unrelated things, it is
  probably two steps. If it exists only to pass a value from one step to the next, it is probably not a
  step at all.</p>

<p>Splitting at that grain pays off twice. The log gains a banner and a result table per action, so a
  failure points at the action that produced it, and each of those actions becomes independently
  reusable - the same step appears in several
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/">flows</a> across different tests.</p>

<h2 id="remote">Steps on a remote endpoint</h2>
<p>When a flow runs a step against a remote endpoint, the framework transfers the test package to that
  endpoint, executes <code>_run_step</code> there, and returns the result to the controller. The step
  code does not know the difference, which means two things in practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Paths inside a step are paths on the machine the step is executing on. Anything a step needs must
    arrive as a package field or through the flow's
    <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/#transfer">transfer</a> helper.</li>
  <li>Anything a step wants to hand to the next step must go through its result, since the two may not be
    running on the same machine.</li>
</ul>

<p><a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Orcraft Endpoints</a> covers dispatch, package upload and
  the endpoint field types in full.</p>

<h2 id="standalone">Running a step directly</h2>
<p>Steps are normally run by a flow through
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/flows/#run-step"><code>run_step</code></a>, which builds the
  step, applies the config values and dispatches it. A step can also be constructed and run directly,
  which is occasionally useful while developing one:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('step = MyStep(
	MyStepConfig(output_folder="/tmp/run", run_id="0001")
)
result = step.run()') }}

<p>A step run this way always runs locally - remote dispatch is something the flow arranges.</p>
